"instead of reading full memory files (~15K tokens). This is the primary "
"recall mechanism."
msgstr ""

#: src/brain/memory.md:36
msgid "Chunk-Hash Caching (v0.3.82)"
msgstr ""

#: src/brain/memory.md:38
msgid ""
"Embedding is skipped for chunks whose content has not changed (#1107). Each "
"chunk carries a hash; on write, only chunks whose hash differs from the "
"stored one are re-embedded. Editing one line of a large brain file no longer "
"re-embeds the whole document."
msgstr ""

#: src/brain/memory.md:40
msgid "Scoped Search (v0.3.80)"
msgstr ""

#: src/brain/memory.md:42
msgid ""
"`memory_search` takes a `scope` that picks which corpus to search (#1020):"
msgstr ""

#: src/brain/memory.md:44
msgid "Scope"
msgstr ""

#: src/brain/memory.md:44
msgid "Corpus"
msgstr ""

#: src/brain/memory.md:44
msgid "Use it for"
msgstr ""

#: src/brain/memory.md:46
msgid "`memory` (default)"
msgstr ""

#: src/brain/memory.md:46
msgid "Daily logs"
msgstr ""

#: src/brain/memory.md:46
msgid "History: what happened, when, what was decided"
msgstr ""

#: src/brain/memory.md:47
msgid "`brain`"
msgstr ""

#: src/brain/memory.md:47
msgid ""
"Brain files (SOUL, USER, AGENTS, TOOLS, CODE, SECURITY, MEMORY, BOOT, "
"HEARTBEAT)"
msgstr ""

#: src/brain/memory.md:47
msgid ""
"Rules and policy: does a rule about this ALREADY exist, and which file owns "
"it"
msgstr ""

#: src/brain/memory.md:48
msgid "`all`"
msgstr ""

#: src/brain/memory.md:48
msgid "Both"
msgstr ""

#: src/brain/memory.md:48
msgid "\"Have I ever written about this anywhere\""
msgstr ""

#: src/brain/memory.md:50
msgid ""
"Picking the wrong scope is the usual reason a search comes back with nothing "
"useful. Daily logs outnumber brain files and reuse the same words for "
"unrelated things, so searching `memory` for a rule usually fails: history "
"outranks policy and you get confident irrelevant hits. Search `brain` before "
"appending any rule: a hit tells you WHICH file owns it, and then "
"`load_brain_file` with a `query` reads the whole section."
msgstr ""

#: src/brain/memory.md:52
msgid "Chunked Retrieval and Index-on-Write (v0.3.80)"
msgstr ""

#: src/brain/memory.md:54
msgid "Memory retrieval was rebuilt around chunking (#998-1002, #1018):"
msgstr ""

#: src/brain/memory.md:56
msgid ""
"**Documents are chunked before embedding**, so chunks past the first are "
"searchable. Previously a long document effectively hid everything after its "
"opening."
msgstr ""

#: src/brain/memory.md:57
msgid ""
"**Lexical hits are narrowed to the matching chunk**, so keyword results "
"point at the relevant slice instead of the whole document."
msgstr ""

#: src/brain/memory.md:58
msgid ""
"**The index updates on write**: memory is searchable immediately, not from a "
"boot-time snapshot. Brain files are indexed into the brain collection on "
"write too."
msgstr ""

#: src/brain/memory.md:59
msgid ""
"**The store resolves per profile**, so profiles never share or cache each "
"other's memory index."
msgstr ""

#: src/brain/memory.md:60
msgid "**The chunker is multi-byte safe**: no more panics on UTF-8 boundaries."
msgstr ""

#: src/brain/memory.md:61
msgid ""
"**MEMORY.md recall is ranked with BM25** instead of shared-word counts "
"(#996), and recall folds Latin diacritics so accented queries match."
msgstr ""

#: src/brain/memory.md:63
msgid "In-tree Memory Store (v0.3.81)"
msgstr ""

#: src/brain/memory.md:65
msgid ""
"The qmd binary dependency is gone (#1032). OpenCrabs now owns its memory "
"store in-tree (`src/memory/`): SQLite FTS5 for lexical search plus vector "
"embeddings in one store, resolved per profile. One fewer external binary to "
"install or break, and the store schema ships with the binary."
msgstr ""

#: src/brain/memory.md:67
msgid "External Index Paths (v0.3.81)"
msgstr ""

#: src/brain/memory.md:69
msgid ""
"Memory search can read indexes that live outside the profile directory "
"(#1051, #1055):"
msgstr ""

#: src/brain/memory.md:71
msgid ""
"```toml\n"
"[memory]\n"
"# Each entry is a bare path string or a { path, pattern } table.\n"
"# Relative paths resolve against the OpenCrabs home (not the session cwd),\n"
"# so the index stays stable across /cd and profile switches.\n"
"extra_paths = [\n"
"  \"~/knowledge/product-docs\",\n"
"  { path = \"~/knowledge/website-mirror\", pattern = \"docs/**/*.md\" },\n"
"]\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/brain/memory.md:84
msgid ""
"Paths indexed into the `external` collection. Bare strings or `{ path, "
"pattern }` tables"
msgstr ""

#: src/brain/memory.md:85
msgid ""
"Glob excludes applied to external indexing, global across all entries "
"(`.git`, `node_modules`, `.env*`, `*.pem`, `.ssh/**`, `*credential*`, ...)"
msgstr ""

#: src/brain/memory.md:86
msgid ""
"Shared/group sessions are **default-deny**: external content stays out of "
"`memory_search` results unless you opt in"
msgstr ""

#: src/brain/memory.md:87
msgid ""
"Seconds between freshness sweeps that pick up added/removed files. Modified "
"files are also caught lazily at search time"
msgstr ""

#: src/brain/memory.md:89
msgid ""
"Indexed files are searchable within one sweep interval (~5 min), no restart "
"needed. Results come back as ranked excerpts with file paths via "
"`memory_search scope=\"external\"`; the agent can then read the full file "
"for verbatim quotes."
msgstr ""

#: src/brain/memory.md:91
msgid ""
"**Gotchas:** UTF-8 text only (binary files are skipped with a warning); HTML "
"files index as raw markup, so mirror sites to `.md` first; nested roots and "
"symlinks are skipped by design; test external scope from an owner session, "
"group sessions return nothing unless you set `external_allowed_in_shared = "
"true`."
msgstr ""

#: src/brain/memory.md:93
msgid "Build a knowledge base from any docs site"
msgstr ""

#: src/brain/memory.md:95
msgid ""
"`web_scrape` sitemap mode pairs with `extra_paths` into a complete, "
"zero-cost pipeline: scrape a site to markdown, index it, then ask questions "
"answered verbatim from those docs (see [Web Scraping](./web-scrape.md)):"
msgstr ""

#: src/brain/memory.md:97
msgid ""
"```toml\n"
"[memory]\n"
"extra_paths = [\"scrapes/docs.example.com\"]\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/brain/memory.md:102
msgid ""
"`web_scrape url=\"https://docs.example.com\" mode=sitemap export=true`: "
"every sitemap page saved as clean markdown under `scrapes/`, local "
"extraction, no AI/API cost (hard cap: 100 pages per scrape)"
msgstr ""

#: src/brain/memory.md:103
msgid ""
"Point `extra_paths` at the scrapes directory (relative paths resolve against "
"the OpenCrabs home)"
msgstr ""

#: src/brain/memory.md:104
msgid "Ask anything: answers are grounded in and quoted from the indexed docs"
msgstr ""

#: src/brain/memory.md:106
msgid "Structural Code Search Upgrades (v0.5.1)"
msgstr ""

#: src/brain/memory.md:108
msgid ""
"The code-graph answers (`who calls X`, `what does X call`, `impact of X`) "
"got four upgrades:"
msgstr ""

#: src/brain/memory.md:110
msgid ""
"**Truncation is now visible.** Caller sets in real repos are unbounded "
"(5,000+ call sites exist), so every structural listing that had to cut ends "
"with a marker line ( _\"… and N more callers of X (re-query with higher n or "
"offset)\"_) and sets beyond 500 collapse into a per-file rollup instead of "
"one-liners. Nothing is silently chopped anymore."
msgstr ""

#: src/brain/memory.md:114
msgid ""
"**Offset pagination.** `memory_search` accepts an `offset` alongside `n` for "
"structural listings, so the full set can be walked page by page. Ranked "
"text/vector results stay rank-truncated: the window applies to listings only."
msgstr ""

#: src/brain/memory.md:117
msgid ""
"**Depth-2 impact chains.** `impact of X` now walks two hops over the "
"call-edge graph, surfacing the callers-of-callers that a direct query misses."
msgstr ""

#: src/brain/memory.md:119
msgid ""
"**Cleaner hits.** External code hits render **repo-relative paths** instead "
"of absolute machine paths, and every `scope=all` hit carries a **corpus "
"provenance tag** (which index the fact came from), so a memory answer and a "
"code-graph answer can be told apart (#89)."
msgstr ""

#: src/brain/memory.md:123
msgid "Embedding Hardening (v0.3.81)"
msgstr ""

#: src/brain/memory.md:125
msgid ""
"Embedding API calls now carry **timeouts**, a **vector gate** (malformed or "
"empty vectors never reach the store), and **non-blocking writes** (embedding "
"failures never stall the turn) (#1062). `/doctor` reports **embedding "
"health**, and a sweep finds **unembedded documents** and backfills them "
"(#1069, #1067)."
msgstr ""

#: src/brain/memory.md:127
msgid "Embedding Modes"
msgstr ""

#: src/brain/memory.md:129
msgid "OpenCrabs supports three embedding configurations:"
msgstr ""

#: src/brain/memory.md:131
msgid ""
"**Local GGUF** (default): downloads a 300MB embedding model and runs it "
"locally via llama.cpp"
msgstr ""

#: src/brain/memory.md:132
msgid ""
"**OpenAI-compatible API**: configure external embedding providers (OpenAI "
"`text-embedding-3-small`, Ollama `nomic-embed-text`, Jina, LM Studio, or any "
"`/v1/embeddings` endpoint) via `[memory.embedding]` config with `url`, "
"`model`, `api_key`, `dimensions`"
msgstr ""

#: src/brain/memory.md:133
msgid ""
"**FTS5-only**: pure keyword search with zero RAM overhead. Set `[memory] "
"vector_enabled = false`. Auto-detects VPS environments and configures "
"automatically"
msgstr ""

#: src/brain/memory.md:135
msgid "Context Compaction"
msgstr ""

#: src/brain/memory.md:137
msgid "When context reaches ~80% capacity, OpenCrabs automatically compacts:"
msgstr ""

#: src/brain/memory.md:139
msgid ""
"Summarizes the conversation so far into a comprehensive continuation document"
msgstr ""

#: src/brain/memory.md:140
msgid "Clears old messages from context"
msgstr ""

#: src/brain/memory.md:141
msgid "Continues with the summary as context"
msgstr ""

#: src/brain/memory.md:143
msgid "Manual compaction: type `/compact` in chat."
msgstr ""

#: src/brain/memory.md:145
msgid "Auto-Save Triggers"
msgstr ""

#: src/brain/memory.md:147
msgid "The agent saves to memory when:"
msgstr ""

#: src/brain/memory.md:148
msgid "New integrations are connected"
msgstr ""

#: src/brain/memory.md:149
msgid "Server/infrastructure changes occur"
msgstr ""

#: src/brain/memory.md:150
msgid "Bugs are found and fixed"
msgstr ""

#: src/brain/memory.md:151
msgid "New tools are configured"
msgstr ""

#: src/brain/memory.md:152
msgid "Credentials are rotated"
msgstr ""

#: src/brain/memory.md:153
msgid "Architecture decisions are made"
msgstr ""

#: src/brain/memory.md:154
msgid "You say \"remember this\""
msgstr ""

#: src/brain/memory.md:155
msgid "Errors take >5 minutes to debug"
msgstr ""

#: src/brain/memory.md:157
msgid "Proactive Memory Surfacing (v0.3.76)"
msgstr ""

#: src/brain/memory.md:159
msgid ""
"The agent now **surfaces relevant memory without being asked**. When a "
"conversation topic matches something stored in MEMORY.md or daily notes, the "
"relevant context is loaded and injected automatically. Previously, memory "
"was only recalled when the agent explicitly called `memory_search` or "
"`load_brain_file`. Now the system proactively checks for relevant context on "
"each turn, so the agent brings up past decisions, server details, or "
"preferences that apply to the current conversation without you having to say "
"\"check your memory.\""
msgstr ""

#: src/brain/memory.md:161
msgid "Confidence, Decay and Pruning (v0.5.3)"
msgstr ""

#: src/brain/memory.md:163
msgid ""
"MEMORY.md facts carry **confidence** and **decay** over time (#1643): a "
"belief's trust drops as it goes unused and unverified, so stale claims stop "
"outranking fresh ones when memory is recalled. Decay is config-driven "
"through the `[epistemic]` section."
msgstr ""

#: src/brain/memory.md:167
msgid "Cold content is managed in two passes (#1657):"
msgstr ""

#: src/brain/memory.md:169
msgid ""
"**Archive pass**: cold MEMORY.md sections move to `memory/archive/` with a "
"backup and reindex, instead of silently accumulating forever."
msgstr ""

#: src/brain/memory.md:171
msgid ""
"**`opencrabs memory prune`**: a dry-run preview (the default) of cold "
"beliefs (key, confidence, hits, age) and cold sections (heading, bytes). "
"`--apply` archives the sections first, then sweeps the remaining cold "
"beliefs. `--max-age-days <N>` overrides the cold threshold (default: 90)."
msgstr ""

#: src/brain/memory.md:178
msgid ""
"See [Brain Files](./brain-files.md) for the full list of files the agent "
"reads on startup."
msgstr ""

#: src/brain/brain-files.md:3
msgid ""
"Brain files define the agent's personality, knowledge, and behavior. They "
"live at `~/.opencrabs/` and are loaded on every session start."
msgstr ""

#: src/brain/brain-files.md:5
msgid "Startup Read Order"
msgstr ""

#: src/brain/brain-files.md:7
msgid "`SOUL.md`: Personality and values"
msgstr ""

#: src/brain/brain-files.md:8
msgid "`USER.md`: Your profile and preferences"
msgstr ""

#: src/brain/brain-files.md:9
msgid "`memory/YYYY-MM-DD.md`: Today's notes"
msgstr ""

#: src/brain/brain-files.md:10
msgid "`MEMORY.md`: Long-term memory"
msgstr ""

#: src/brain/brain-files.md:11
msgid "`AGENTS.md`: Agent behavior guidelines"
msgstr ""

#: src/brain/brain-files.md:12
msgid "`TOOLS.md`: Tool reference and custom notes"
msgstr ""

#: src/brain/brain-files.md:13
msgid "`CODE.md`: Coding standards and file organization"
msgstr ""

#: src/brain/brain-files.md:14
msgid "`SECURITY.md`: Security policies"
msgstr ""

#: src/brain/brain-files.md:15
msgid "`HEARTBEAT.md`: Periodic check tasks"
msgstr ""

#: src/brain/brain-files.md:17
msgid "File Reference"
msgstr ""

#: src/brain/brain-files.md:19
msgid "SOUL.md"
msgstr ""

#: src/brain/brain-files.md:20
msgid ""
"Agent personality. Core truths: strong opinions, brevity, resourcefulness, "
"honesty. Hard rules: never delete files without approval, never send emails "
"without request, never commit code directly."
msgstr ""

#: src/brain/brain-files.md:22
msgid "USER.md"
msgstr ""

#: src/brain/brain-files.md:23
msgid ""
"Your profile: name, location, timezone, role, specialties, communication "
"preferences, pet peeves."
msgstr ""

#: src/brain/brain-files.md:25
msgid "AGENTS.md"
msgstr ""

#: src/brain/brain-files.md:26
msgid ""
"Comprehensive agent behavior docs: memory system, safety rules, git rules, "
"workspace vs repository separation, cron best practices, platform "
"formatting, heartbeat guidelines."
msgstr ""

#: src/brain/brain-files.md:28
msgid "TOOLS.md"
msgstr ""

#: src/brain/brain-files.md:29
msgid ""
"Tool parameter reference, system CLI tools, provider configuration, "
"integration details for all channels and services."
msgstr ""

#: src/brain/brain-files.md:31
msgid "CODE.md"
msgstr ""

#: src/brain/brain-files.md:32
msgid ""
"Coding standards brain template. Enforces: no file over 500 lines (target "
"100–250), types in `types.rs`, one responsibility per file, mandatory tests "
"for every feature, security-first patterns. Rust-first philosophy, single "
"binary, no runtime dependencies. The agent follows these rules when writing "
"or reviewing code."
msgstr ""

#: src/brain/brain-files.md:34
msgid "SECURITY.md"
msgstr ""

#: src/brain/brain-files.md:35
msgid ""
"Security policies: third-party code review, attack playbook awareness, "
"network security, data handling, incident response."
msgstr ""

#: src/brain/brain-files.md:37
msgid "HEARTBEAT.md"
msgstr ""

#: src/brain/brain-files.md:38
msgid ""
"Tasks for periodic proactive checks. Keep empty to skip heartbeat API calls. "
"Add tasks for the agent to rotate through (email checks, calendar, weather, "
"etc.)."
msgstr ""

#: src/brain/brain-files.md:40
msgid "BOOT.md"
msgstr ""

#: src/brain/brain-files.md:41
msgid ""
"Startup procedures: check git log, verify build, greet human with context "
"awareness."
msgstr ""

#: src/brain/brain-files.md:43
msgid "Query-Based Loading (v0.3.76)"
msgstr ""

#: src/brain/brain-files.md:45
msgid ""
"`load_brain_file` now accepts an optional `query` parameter that returns "
"**only the sections matching the query** instead of the entire file. This "
"saves significant context budget:"
msgstr ""

#: src/brain/brain-files.md:47
msgid ""
"```\n"
"load_brain_file(name=\"TOOLS.md\", query=\"telegram\")\n"
"→ Returns only the Telegram-related sections, not the full 2000-line file\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/brain/brain-files.md:52
msgid ""
"Whole sections are returned (never cut mid-section), so a rule is never "
"truncated. This is especially useful for large brain files like TOOLS.md and "
"AGENTS.md where loading the full file wastes 10K+ tokens on irrelevant "
"content."
msgstr ""

#: src/brain/brain-files.md:54
msgid "Customization"
msgstr ""

#: src/brain/brain-files.md:56
msgid ""
"These files are **yours**. The agent reads them but you control the content. "
"Templates are at `src/docs/reference/templates/` in the source repo, compare "
"your local files against templates when updating to pick up new sections "
"without losing custom content."
msgstr ""

#: src/brain/brain-files.md:58
msgid ""
"**New installs (v0.2.72+):** CODE.md and SECURITY.md are automatically "
"seeded on first run. Existing users can ask their crab: _\"Check my brain "
"templates and update them if any are missing or outdated.\"_"
msgstr ""

#: src/brain/brain-files.md:60
msgid ""
"**Upgrading:** Brain files are never overwritten by `/evolve` or `/rebuild`. "
"After updating, ask your crab to compare templates against local files and "
"patch in new sections."
msgstr ""

#: src/brain/brain-files.md:62
msgid "User-owned files are never synced (v0.3.82)"
msgstr ""

#: src/brain/brain-files.md:64
msgid ""
"`SOUL.md`, `USER.md` and `MEMORY.md` are **excluded from upstream template "
"sync** (#1119). They hold your agent's personality, your identity, and what "
"it has learned about you, so upstream has no authority over them. Before "
"this, every startup appended upstream sections into them, which grew one "
"user's SOUL.md from under 2 KB to nearly 5 KB, re-appending after each "
"manual prune."
msgstr ""

#: src/brain/brain-files.md:66
msgid ""
"Seeding is unaffected: all three are still created on first run. Only the "
"merge is forbidden. The remaining brain files (AGENTS, TOOLS, CODE, "
"SECURITY, BOOT, HEARTBEAT) continue to sync normally."
msgstr ""

#: src/brain/brain-files.md:68
msgid ""
"Related (#1121): rules belong in `AGENTS.md`, not `MEMORY.md`. MEMORY.md is "
"on-demand, reached only through `memory_search`, so a rule written there "
"does not bind on a cold session and does not survive compaction. Facts go in "
"MEMORY.md; anything that must always hold goes in AGENTS.md, which is always "
"loaded."
msgstr ""

#: src/features/sessions.md:3
msgid "Sessions as Isolated Agents"
msgstr ""

#: src/features/sessions.md:5
msgid ""
"A session in OpenCrabs is not a tab, not a window, not a chat thread. **Each "
"session is a fully independent agent** with its own brain: conversation "
"history, provider, model, working directory, tool state, approval policy, "
"and context window. When you create a new session, you are spinning up a "
"separate agent that knows nothing about any other session and shares nothing "
"with them."
msgstr ""

#: src/features/sessions.md:7
msgid ""
"This is the core mental model: **one session = one agent = one context**. "
"You can run dozens of sessions in parallel and they will never interfere "
"with each other."
msgstr ""

#: src/features/sessions.md:9
msgid "Zero Context Contamination"
msgstr ""

#: src/features/sessions.md:11
msgid "Sessions are completely isolated at every layer:"
msgstr ""

#: src/features/sessions.md:13
msgid ""
"**Separate message queues**: each session has its own queue. Messages are "
"routed strictly to their originating session. No cross-session bleeding, "
"even when 10 sessions are processing simultaneously."
msgstr ""

#: src/features/sessions.md:14
msgid ""
"**Separate provider and model**: switching to Gemini in session A does not "
"affect session B running Claude. Each session remembers its own provider "
"independently."
msgstr ""

#: src/features/sessions.md:15
msgid ""
"**Separate working directory**: `/cd` in one session does not change the "
"working directory of any other session."
msgstr ""

#: src/features/sessions.md:16
msgid ""
"**Separate conversation history**: full SQLite-backed history per session. "
"No shared memory, no prompt pollution, no context bleed."
msgstr ""

#: src/features/sessions.md:17
msgid ""
"**Separate token tracking**: cumulative usage, cost, and context window are "
"tracked per session."
msgstr ""

#: src/features/sessions.md:19
msgid ""
"This isolation is guaranteed by **Rust's thread safety and async runtime**. "
"Each session runs as an independent `tokio` task with its own state, and the "
"type system prevents accidental sharing at compile time. You can run split "
"panes, channel sessions, background agents, and sub-agents all at once with "
"zero risk of one session's output leaking into another."
msgstr ""

#: src/features/sessions.md:21
msgid "Switching Sessions Keeps the Transcript"
msgstr ""

#: src/features/sessions.md:23
msgid ""
"Switching sessions with `/sessions` and coming back does not fold your "
"transcript away. The fold state for every turn is keyed by a stable role and "
"timestamp anchor, so a turn you had opened stays open across a reload, and a "
"turn you had folded stays folded (#2155). A turn that is still running also "
"stays unfolded while it runs, so the work in flight stays visible instead of "
"hiding behind a collapsed header."
msgstr ""

#: src/features/sessions.md:25
msgid ""
"The display window keeps a bounded number of recent messages in the pane "
"rather than trimming by token budget, so one huge tool result can no longer "
"evict the rest of the conversation. The hidden-count marker still tells you "
"how many older rows are out of view, and Ctrl+O pages back through them."
msgstr ""

#: src/features/sessions.md:27
msgid "Workflow Patterns: One Session Per Context"
msgstr ""

#: src/features/sessions.md:29
msgid ""
"The power of isolated sessions becomes clear when you treat each one as a "
"dedicated agent for a specific domain:"
msgstr ""

#: src/features/sessions.md:31
msgid "Pattern 1: DevOps for a Server"
msgstr ""

#: src/features/sessions.md:33
msgid ""
"Create a session for a specific server or infrastructure concern. Send a "
"first message like `\"Devops for server XYZ, monitor nginx builds, manage "
"cronjobs, handle deployments, run log cleanups\"`. The session locks into "
"that context: working directory set to the server's codebase, provider set "
"to a fast model for quick ops tasks, history filled with that server's "
"deployment patterns. Come back to it days later and the agent remembers "
"every previous deploy, every cron change, every nginx config tweak."
msgstr ""

#: src/features/sessions.md:35
msgid "Pattern 2: Mobile App Development with a Co-Founder"
msgstr ""

#: src/features/sessions.md:37
msgid ""
"Create a session named `mybrand-mobile` and connect it to a Telegram group "
"with your co-founder. The agent is locked into the Dart/Flutter codebase, "
"the product design context, and the mobile-specific toolchain. Your "
"co-founder can ask questions, request design changes, or review PRs directly "
"in Telegram while you work on backend tasks in a separate session. The two "
"contexts never mix."
msgstr ""

#: src/features/sessions.md:39
msgid "Pattern 3: Production Logs with a Team on Slack"
msgstr ""

#: src/features/sessions.md:41
msgid ""
"Create a session named `mybrand-prod-logs-debug` and connect it to a Slack "
"channel. Your team can ask questions about production, staging, or dev logs "
"without you having to context-switch. The agent stays locked into log "
"analysis mode with the right SSH aliases, the right log paths, and the right "
"debugging tools. Meanwhile, your main TUI session is free for development "
"work."
msgstr ""

#: src/features/sessions.md:43
msgid ""
"The key insight: **you never have to explain the full context again**. Once "
"a session is locked into a domain, every follow-up message inherits that "
"context automatically."
msgstr ""

#: src/features/sessions.md:45
msgid "Session Context Integrity (v0.3.78)"
msgstr ""

#: src/features/sessions.md:47
msgid ""
"Two fixes harden `session_context` (the tool agents use to store key-value "
"state):"
msgstr ""

#: src/features/sessions.md:49
msgid ""
"**Atomic writes**: context writes now use atomic file operations, "
"eliminating trailing-character corruption that could occur when a write was "
"interrupted mid-flush. Previously, a crashed write could leave a JSON file "
"with a truncated trailing character, making the entire context unreadable."
msgstr ""

#: src/features/sessions.md:50
msgid ""
"**Concurrency pinning**: concurrent `session_context` calls from parallel "
"tool executions no longer race on the same file. A concurrency pin "
"serializes writes, and temp files are cleaned up after each operation."
msgstr ""

#: src/features/sessions.md:52
msgid "Creating Sessions"
msgstr ""

